WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:03.865
&gt;&gt; Change lives.

00:00:03.865 --> 00:00:05.943
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Change organizations.

00:00:05.943 --> 00:00:08.542
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Change the world.

00:00:17.424 --> 00:00:27.424
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:00:39.402 --> 00:00:43.360
Does my singing that song cross a line for
you?

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.410
Now what if I told you that while for

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:53.140
some that song recalls moonlight
magnolias, and mint juleps?

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:02.010
For most people is a rallying cry for
racism and bigotry.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.921
Sung at the turn of
the century in black face.

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:11.380
Does it a cross a line for you now?

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.000
Now most of the time when we think
about crossing lines, it's a pinch

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.540
at best And at worst, it's offensive or
inciting of violence.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.140
But what I'm here today
to talk to you about

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:35.870
is how crossing lines can actually end
the prejudice that that song belies.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.160
Now, you may be asking yourself,
who am I to talk about crossing lines,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.980
because I've got lines
drawn all around me.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.176
But, as Cal Robin has taught
us in touchy-feely so

00:01:53.176 --> 00:01:56.160
well, I am now going to name my lines for
you.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.649
Some that you can see,
and some that you can't.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.440
So I am white, I am female,

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.210
I am upper-middle class, I am Christian,

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:15.260
and as my accent belies, I am Southern.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.900
And so what that means, for me and
my life, is that I have a family and

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.500
a history of slave ownership.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:32.440
Who am I to talk about prejudice and
crossing lines?

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.530
And you know moreover,

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:38.030
civil rights movement happened 40
years ago, we have a black president.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:40.190
Let's move on.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.310
I'm here today to talk about
why we can't move on, because

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:52.250
while a lot of my stories and
my personal history deal with racism,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:00.570
I'm gonna argue today that there is
prejudice right here in our communities.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.080
At the GSB.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.870
And, I'm sure in the communities
of professional circles that you,

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:10.470
as alumni, also work in.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:15.020
So the first line that I'm gonna
start at is a tennis base line.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.580
And that's in my home town of Lake City,
South Carolina.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.100
The most beautiful place in the world.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.640
Neither a lake, nor a city, discuss.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.402
So if you want to go to Lake City in
1998 and visit this tennis base line,

00:03:33.402 --> 00:03:36.770
at the Lions Park across
the street from my house.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:42.460
You would find me there every afternoon
after school with my best friend, Tony.

00:03:43.615 --> 00:03:46.860
Now, Tony wanted to learn
how to play Tennis.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.930
And I had played being, a white upper
middle class woman for a long time.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.300
And so we chased balls around most days.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.340
But hopefully that would be enough.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:02.550
And so this afternoon,
like every other, we had our practice.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.620
And then I went to my grandmother's
house like I did every night for dinner.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.320
And as I was sitting
down at the dinner table.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.610
My grandmother said I got
a phone call today from Teresa.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.560
Now, I want everybody to get in their
minds, the snoodiest person that you know.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:28.310
Then I want to multiply that by ten,
sprinkle just a little bit of disdain for

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:32.860
color, tip up your nose,
and that is Teresa.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:36.780
And Teresa had called my grandmother.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.940
And said that she had seen me
playing tennis with Tony and

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:46.910
that I as a white, woman, Christian,
upper class, I should know better.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.710
Because you see what I didn't
tell you is that Tony is black.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:55.180
Shouldn't do it.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.370
And as these words started coming
out of my grandmother's mouth,

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:01.820
I couldn't believe it.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.880
All that I had heard from her,
in my life, was love and

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.540
acceptance and
this was hatred and bigotry.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:16.570
And so if you know me, as many in
the room do, I'm a little bit dramatic.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:17.690
So, I had a dramatic moment.

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.400
I got up from the table and I ran.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:27.450
I did not want to be in the same space as
these words so that they might infect me.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:33.350
And over the next days my grandmother
tried to repair that relationship.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:37.230
She came over to my house,
with her a blue velvet box.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.490
And, inside was a cubic zirconium cross.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:43.760
She was going to buy me back.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.290
And I remember looking at the symbol
that for me was faith, hope and

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.880
love and all I saw was hatred and
and racism.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.340
But, over the next five years,

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:04.430
there's some thesis work
that I did on my hometown.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.810
I had the great fortune of meeting Paula.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.473
Now Paula,
was a pillar in her church community,

00:06:11.473 --> 00:06:14.904
the Wesley United Methodist Church,
in my hometown of Lake City,

00:06:14.904 --> 00:06:16.880
an African-American church there.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.521
And Paula and I got very close as a part
of this work, and because of that,

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:28.934
Paula said that she was gonna come to
the bible study at my church, 7:00 a.m.

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:31.230
On Wednesday morning.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.940
And as she walked into
the room that morning,

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.630
there were a whole lot of
Theresa's around the table.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.010
And they scooted to the other side.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.330
Except for one.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.710
My grandmother.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:48.100
Because over the course
of those five years,

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:52.730
she had been walking slowly over the line.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.730
Because what she saw there was that Paula,
was indeed black.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:06:59.776
But she was also a woman.

00:06:59.776 --> 00:07:01.220
She was a Christian.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:05.310
She was a grandmother, she was a mother.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:09.080
All the things that my
grandmother was herself.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:14.772
And a few years ago, we threw a surprise
birthday party for my grandmother for

00:07:14.772 --> 00:07:15.865
her 86th birthday.

00:07:19.104 --> 00:07:21.530
The first person there was Paula.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:27.080
And as she walked across a threshold
at the front door of my grandmother's

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:28.080
rambling Southern house.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.410
She was doing more than just
walking across a door frame.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:37.360
She was walking across
100 years of history.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.560
Where in that house black people were
not allowed to enter the front door.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:53.130
And as she did that, she was met
with an embrace on the other side.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:08:00.100
Two lines were crossed that day and
the history of hatred began to fade away.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:06.870
Now you might be saying this only happens
in backwater, Southern towns like yours.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.610
I admit, I am from the backwater.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:14.710
But I'm gonna argue that it
happens right here at the GSB.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:18.830
So when we apply here,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.760
Derek Bolton has selected us on
some fabulous characteristics.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.460
We are achievers.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:28.310
We are excellent.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:31.280
We are multi-cultural.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:37.240
But time as we get here, we get out our
markers and we start drawing our lines.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:41.450
Went on the Colombia trip before school.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.576
Didn't go on the Colombia
trip before school.

00:08:44.576 --> 00:08:47.738
Investment banker.

00:08:47.738 --> 00:08:49.910
Non-profit.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.470
International.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:54.580
American.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.030
We segment and super-segment and
sub-segment because it feels good.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.820
It feels good to be with
people who are like us.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:09.950
But, what does that say about
the community we create?

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:15.330
So I wanna tell you about a time
when I left my markers at home,

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:19.560
mostly because I was running
to the airport and I was late.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.960
And that was when I came to,
Admit Weekend, two years ago,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.680
and that's where I met Sabah.

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:34.800
Now because of some bright
person in the Admissions Office,

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.600
we had been put together in
one of those dinners that you

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.260
have to sort of get to know your
potential future classmates.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.480
And if I'd had my markers out that day,

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.860
here are the lines that would have
been set up around us to separate us.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.450
Sabah is from Cyprus.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.170
I'm from South Carolina.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:58.410
Sabah is a scientist.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:03.260
I was a marketer.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:04.170
Sabah is an atheist.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:06.340
I'm a Christian.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:14.580
But fortunate, I think for
me, what I saw that day and

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:20.000
learned was that Sabah
likes really good coffee.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.189
He's a bit of a snob that way,
just like me.

00:10:22.189 --> 00:10:29.138
And Sabah really loves the best barbecue
that you can find, just like me.

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.830
Before the GSB,
Sabah lived in North Carolina.

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:39.770
Not South Carolina, but
we'll give him that one.

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:42.080
We'll let that one slide.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:48.690
I was able to see, lines and all, the full
person across the table from me that day.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:56.850
And I think about the Wednesday mornings
that we've had breakfast together now for

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.850
almost two years.

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:01.588
And I think about if that
hadn't been there in my life,

00:11:01.588 --> 00:11:03.202
that I wouldn't be right here.

00:11:06.081 --> 00:11:08.290
And so I've told you lots of stories.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:09.420
What can we do?

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.370
To cross the lines during our lives.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:16.420
I'm going to give you three ways.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:24.180
Way number one is to really, touchy-feeley
style, meet people where they are.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.980
Now what does that look like?

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:35.130
It means sitting across the table and
hearing the story, and sharing your own.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:39.930
Not to judge or to question or
to argue, just to sit and be.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.950
It worked out when I met Sabah.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:47.580
I'll tell you when it didn't work
out was with my grandmother.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.380
Because, in that exchange,
she was wrong, I was right.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:54.110
I didn't want to hear it.

00:11:55.930 --> 00:12:00.100
But, I reflect on that now and
I think what if I had asked the questions.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:05.719
I would have probably learned that she
had been told by people that she loved,

00:12:05.719 --> 00:12:07.888
that black people were less.

00:12:10.002 --> 00:12:13.469
I think we would've come
to the same conclusion, but

00:12:13.469 --> 00:12:16.790
maybe we'd have gotten
there a little bit faster.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:19.835
Maybe it wouldn't have taken five years.

00:12:19.835 --> 00:12:20.500
Maybe it would've taken one.

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:26.420
Maybe it would've taken just
the hour to have the conversation.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.250
So, meet people where they are.

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:36.490
The second way, is to really be ready for

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:42.070
the chance encounter that some magic
person in an admissions office,

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:45.924
or some diversity committee, or
some team manager creates for you.

00:12:45.924 --> 00:12:52.620
And how do you open yourself for
that kind of readiness?

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:55.750
It's really about self-reflection.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.980
You have to know where the lines
are in your own life so

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:04.430
that you can be ready, willing,
and able to cross them.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:08.210
Which leads me to my last reason.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.590
Which is really the most important.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:17.720
Is that to allow someone else to dictate
diversity in your own life is not enough.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.580
You have to make your own dinner tables.

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.320
So in closing I wanna leave
you with another song.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:33.527
Interestingly enough it was
written at the same time as Dixie,

00:13:33.527 --> 00:13:37.489
but it has what I think is
a bit of a better message.

00:13:37.489 --> 00:13:42.219
And that's really
the essence of community,

00:13:42.219 --> 00:13:45.744
which I would argue Is communion.

00:13:45.744 --> 00:13:55.744
[MUSIC]

00:14:19.354 --> 00:14:21.490
Who will you break bread with tonight?

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:22.930
Thank you very much.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.460
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

